ICOR Women, the women’s section of the International Coordination of Revolutionary Parties and Organizations (ICOR), expressed support for the international solidarity campaign launched in April in support of the Kurdish Women’s Protection Units (YPJ).


ICOR Women said that the YPJ is not merely a military organization, but represents women’s right to self-defense, equality, and to determine their own social lives.


The statement said that for more than 13 years, the YPJ has represented women’s resistance to patriarchal violence and oppression, adding that Kurdish women’s struggle for freedom has served as an example to women around the world.


The statement also referred to the agreement signed between the Syrian government and the Syrian Democratic Forces (SDF) on January 29, 2026, saying that a new period has begun for the future of Syria and Rojava. However, it emphasized that in negotiations concerning the future of a new Syria, women’s participation in political, social, and military life must not be treated as a secondary issue.


“You cannot eliminate the YPJ through negotiations!” the statement said, describing the YPJ as representing self-determination, equality, and the protection of women from violence and oppression. It called for the gains achieved by women in Rojava not to be lost in a new Syria, and for the YPJ’s existence to be institutionally recognized and protected.


The statement noted that attacks and threats against women continue and said that the need for self-defense has not disappeared. On the contrary, it argued that self-defense must be institutionally and legally guaranteed in order to protect women’s gains.


The statement also claimed that the HTS (Hay'at Tahrir al-Sham), which is currently in power in Syria, seeks to exclude women from various areas of life, particularly the military sphere, and said that democracy, peace, and equality are impossible without women’s equal participation.


The demands of the international solidarity campaign are as follows:


- Recognition of the YPJ by the Syrian Ministry of Defense;


- Preservation of the existing structure of women’s defense units in the regions of Rojava;


- Protection and safeguarding of the gains achieved by women in Rojava;


- Release of imprisoned women, the return of the remains of women who have died, and clarification of the fate of missing women;


- Support from relevant international powers for the integration of women’s defense units;


- Strengthening women’s participation in military and security institutions.


At the end of the statement, ICOR Women said they supported the international solidarity campaign and its demands, stating: “Self-defense is a natural right. Women must have the right to defend their own lives, freedom, and communities themselves.”
